B DPO experiment, the median tier scenario shows this wasn't completed—the finding remains 7B-only. Since the user is asking for detailed predictions across all planned experiments, I should note that this one didn't run in the median scenario, though I could still project what would happen if it had.\n\nNow I'm thinking through plan_005b's multi-run replication structure. I need to estimate what individual run results would look like—each run would show variation in politeness and completion rates across the different conditions, and then I'd aggregate those four runs to get the median tier outcomes.\n\nFor the bootstrap CI experiment in plan_005, I'm working with the predefined confidence interval ranges to set realistic point estimates and bounds that fit within the median tier constraints.\n\nNow I'm thinking through what the outputs should look like across the different plans—per-run results tables and box plots for plan_005b, bootstrap CI tables and forest plots for plan_005, and plan_006 isn't ready yet.\n\nConstructing the YAML output...\n\nActually, I need to be careful about how plan_005b layers on top of the existing data. The original run is my baseline, and I'm adding three new runs that should combine to produce the median-tier results I'm targeting. Let me work out concrete numbers for those new runs that, when aggregated with the original, hit the right outcomes.\n\nNow I'm laying out the specific metrics for runs 2, 3, and 4 across all the different conditions—completion rates and politeness scores for baseline, dialect variants, overcorrected speech, and the single-dimension manipulations. The overcorrected condition consistently shows a politeness gap of around 3-4.5 percentage points above baseline, which is what I'm aiming for in the aggregate.\n\nLooking at the pattern across all four runs, the overcorrection rebound is holding steady at +3.4pp on average, with all four runs moving in the same direction. But I'm also factoring in the hgate-dpo0