nsistently demonstrates completion collapse across all runs with the same directional effect, confirming it's not a fluke despite the wide confidence intervals, while FM2's politeness arm needs closer examination.\n\nLooking at the multi-run results, FM2 replicates in most runs but the question_rate arm doesn't reach significance, so I'm downgrading FM2 to only the politeness mechanism. FM3 shows consistent positive overcorrection rebound around 3-5pp across runs, and the H-gate tests show H1 passing all runs while H2 and H3 fail consistently. For the bootstrap analysis, FM4's reversal effect sits around +12pp with a 95% CI of [+2, +32], which excludes zero and suggests directional support.\n\nThe FM3 rebound estimate is +7.2pp with a CI of [+0.5, +14.0]—the lower bound being near zero tells me the pattern itself is more reliable than the magnitude. The scale completion difference between 14B and 7B models shows a clear +24.8pp advantage for the larger model with a tight CI of [+6, +44]. However, H2's best gap reduction only reaches 23% with a 95% CI of [18%, 28%], falling short of the 30% threshold needed for calibration to pass. Plan_006 couldn't run due to GPU constraints, so C2 remains a 7B-only finding that I'll need to flag explicitly in scope.\n\nNow I'm structuring the full YAML output with tier assignments, narrative guidance for each finding, and the imagined user context.\n\nFor the imagined experiments section, I need to detail each planned experiment with its name, plan ID, description, expected results, and the reasoning behind those expectations, plus the tables and figures I'd generate and how I'd analyze them. Let me work through plan_005b first by laying out the actual per-run metrics from the baseline and each intervention condition. Looking at the performance metrics across these additional runs, I'm seeing how the different variants perform across politeness, question rate, and completion metrics. The single_dim_politeness variant consistently shows higher politeness scores, while single_dim_question tends to have elevated question rates but lower politeness. Now I'm finishing the Indian completion calculation and moving into the overcorrected metrics, then checking how well each variant preserves the baseline completion rate—AAVE is dropping to just under 29% retention while Indian holds at 43%, and I need to compute the mean across all calibrated variants to see which performs best.\n\nFor FM2, I'm looking at how the single dimension politeness question shifts the overall politeness score down by about 10 percentage points relative to baseline, and checking the corresponding question rate metrics.\n\nNow examining FM3, I'm calculating the overcorrection rebound effect across each run—the difference between the politeness score and a reference point—which averages around 3.55 percentage points across the four runs.\n\nFor the bootstrap confidence intervals, I need to pull from all the existing experimental data across multiple plans rather than just the current one. The critical comparison is FM4's reversal: the base model shows the correct direction for the AAVE politeness gap at -0.091, but after DPO training it flips to +0.064, which is the opposite of what we want.\n\nThe concerning part is that this reversal isn't consistent across runs—the per-run gaps in the DPO model vary wildly from -2.0pp to +13.4pp, meaning one run doesn't even exhibit the reversal at all. That's substantial variance to account for when computing the confidence intervals.\n\nLooking at the Indian English gaps, I'm seeing a similar pattern of instability: ranging from -2.2pp to +10.4pp across the four runs. When I pool both dialect groups together to match the paper's framing, the combined gaps show the same kind of volatility, which suggests the mean reversal effect might be less robust than the aggregate numbers initially suggested.\n\nThe bootstrap confidence interval I'm calculating comes out much narrower than the [+2pp, +3