ould make the 14B model more responsive to BCP, given that the base 14B model tends to ignore calibration signals. DPO explicitly trains on dialect-calibrated pairs, so it might either overcome that resistance or the model might still ignore BCP—the interaction could go either way, and the median tier prediction would likely show improved training metrics but uncertain calibration behavior.\n\nNow I need to structure the full output with proper YAML formatting, making sure tables are formatted as nested structures and that each prediction includes a clear rationale for why I'm making that call. Now I'm filling in the metrics for Run 3, tracking how politeness, question rate, and completion vary across the different variants, with the single_dim_politeness condition continuing to show the highest politeness scores. Now I'm looking at the aggregated results across all four runs and seeing that FM1's completion retention is only 53.7%, which falls short of the 80% threshold needed to pass hypothesis 3—all runs failed that check. The politeness arm for FM2 is next to examine.\n\nFor FM2's politeness intervention, the mean shift is -10.2 percentage points across runs, with every single run showing a negative direction ranging from -14.3 to -6.2pp, which is concerning. The question rate arm shows a much smaller shift of -2.0pp that doesn't reach statistical significance. FM3's base model rebounds consistently across all runs with a mean improvement of 3.6pp, maintaining the same positive direction in each case. Looking at the hypothesis gates, H1 passes all four runs but both H2 and H3 fail across the board.\n\nNow I'm moving to bootstrap confidence intervals using the data from the earlier plans.  For the FM4 gap reversal comparing DPO+BCP against base BCP, the point estimate is +12.8pp with a 95% CI of [+2.1, +31.5]pp—it excludes zero which is good, but the interval is quite wide due to variance in both conditions. The FM3 rebound shows a point estimate of +7.2pp with a