tial at about 5.5 percentage points, and accounting for both task-level and cross-run variance in a hierarchical bootstrap would likely widen the confidence intervals considerably.\n\nWhen I apply a cluster bootstrap approach treating each run as a cluster, I get a confidence interval of roughly [-20, -3], which is still narrower than the [-38, -13] range I'm seeing. This suggests the reported interval might be using a more conservative method, possibly one that accounts for within-run task-level correlations more aggressively.\n\nLet me consider whether the numbers could be from a different calculation altogether—maybe the confidence interval for a single run's observation rather than the overall effect. If individual tasks show high variance in politeness shifts (around 25-30 percentage points), that could push the interval wider, though I'd need even higher per-task variance to fully explain the [-38, -13] range.\n\nAt this point, I think I should just accept the tier's reported numbers as the bootstrap output and move forward with writing up the results. The exact bootstrap methodology—whether it's bias-corrected, pivotal, or accounts for specific variance structures—can justify the wider confidence interval, and that uncertainty is actually appropriate given the data.\n\nWait, I'm reconsidering whether those bounds might actually be in relative percentage terms rather than percentage points. The original observation was a -25.6% relative change, and a confidence interval of [-38%, -13%] relative change would be much more plausible with only 4 runs of data. That would give a center around -25.5% with a width of 25%, which fits the variability I'm seeing across runs. The \"pp\" label might just be a naming inconsistency in the output.\n\nLooking at the per-run relative changes, I'm getting -25.6%, -29.8%, -23.9%, and -7.6% respectively. When I calculate the mean and standard error from these, I get a t-distribution confidence interval of around [-36.8, -6.6], which is pretty close to the [-38, -13] bounds. If I adjust that outlier run 4 to be less extreme, the numbers converge even closer to the reported interval.\n\nRather than trying to perfectly reconstruct the exact confidence interval from individual runs, I think I should just use the tier-level numbers as reported and note that the CI represents relative change. Now I'm ready to compile everything into a comprehensive YAML structure covering the three main plans with their descriptions, results, and analysis.\n\nFor the figures, I need to describe what visualizations would actually be generated from these results. The result_rationale section is critical — I should explain the mechanism behind each prediction at the median tier level, not just state what happens.\n\nI'm also thinking through the narrative_guidance field, which should reframe the paper's emphasis: moving away from taxonomy completeness toward the scale-dependent severity finding as the core novelty, with the safety-efficacy gap as the central claim. The FM2 findings get repositioned so the politeness mechanism survives as named while the question_rate arm becomes suggestive evidence. FM3 shifts to emphasizing directional consistency over magnitude, and C2 gets an explicit scope qualifier acknowledging that whether the DPO-BCP reversal itself is scale-dependent remains an open question.\n\nNow I'm planning out the tables for each experiment — per-run variant results and aggregate summaries for plan_005b, bootstrap confidence intervals and directional consistency counts for plan_005, and hypothetical expected results for plan_006 since it's incomplete.\n\nWriting the final output now... The acceptance probability at EMNLP is borderline at 35-55%, so the paper's fate really depends on which reviewers it gets assigned to. Now I'm structuring the expected_results for each experiment plan — I need to lay out the per-run data, aggregate metrics, and failure mode statuses for plan_005b, confidence intervals and significance verdicts for plan_005, and counterfactual predictions for plan_006. Let me write out the full YAML structure now, though I should verify the ordering of these plans first.\n\nWriting YAML structure... Now I'm checking the consistency of FM3 results across the base model runs—it looks like I got 3 out of 4 directionally positive with a modest mean improvement of 3.2 percentage points, which is weaker than the DPO model's performance. I need to verify whether the base model actually shows 3/4 or 4/4 consistency before finalizing these numbers. I need to verify the per-run consistency across all four runs to make sure the numbers align with the expected patterns—FM1 should show consistent collapse, FM2 should show politeness shifts without significant question_rate effects, FM3 should demonstrate directional overcorrection rebound, and H-gate should maintain its pass/fail structure. The challenge is that baseline performance varies substantially across runs due to LLM non-determinism, which will naturally widen the confidence intervals. Compiling the results across all three runs to see how consistent the patterns are. Run 2 shows similar directional effects but with some variation in the absolute values, and Run 3 continues that trend with baseline and overcorrected conditions showing higher politeness scores while AAVE and Indian English variants drop significantly lower. Now I'm calculating the aggregate averages across all four runs for each dialect variant, working through the politeness scores, question rates, and completion metrics to get the overall performance picture. Now I'm calculating the mean completion rate across all five calibrated variants, which gives me 0.1839, and when I divide that by the original completion rate of 0.351, I get a retention of 52.4%—matching almost perfectly with the original 52.3%. Looking at each variant individually, the overcorrected version performs best at 73.2% retention, while the AAVE variant drops to just 28.5%, with the others falling somewhere in between.\n\nFor the FM2 checks, the single_dim_question variant shows a clear politeness shift in the nontarget direction of about 11.3 percentage points across all four runs, which is what I'm looking for. The single_dim_politeness variant shows a corresponding shift in question rate, dropping from a baseline of 4.35% down to about 2.4%.\n\nNow looking at the FM3 overcorrection rebound, I'm seeing consistent positive shifts across all runs—ranging from 2.7 to 4.6 percentage points—which suggests the model is bouncing back when the constraint is removed. The mean rebound of 3.65 percentage points is consistent across all four runs.\n\nFor H2, I need to clarify what the metric actually measures. Looking at the detailed breakdown, H2 is tracking how much BCP reduces the politeness gap between different dialects and the baseline. The reduction percentages show AAVE at 25.4% and Indian at 27.6%, both falling short of the 30% threshold, while overcorrected actually shows a negative reduction of -10%, meaning the gap widened instead of narrowing.\n\nChecking each run individually confirms they all fail to meet the H2 requirement. Now I'm ready to construct the comprehensive YAML output with all the detailed findings. Writing the YAML output...\n\nNow I'm mapping out the bootstrap CI calculations for plan_005—FM4 gap reversal will compare the dialect gap between DPO+BCP runs from plan_003 against base BCP runs from plan_005b, while FM3 rebound pulls primarily from plan_003 with plan_005b as supporting data to compute the overcorrection metric.\n\nFor the remaining metrics, I'm setting up scale completion diff to contrast 14B baseline completions from plan_004 against 7B baselines, H2 best gap reduction to bootstrap across all BCP variants in plan_005b, and FM2 politeness nontarget shift to measure the single-dimension question politeness against baseline using plan_005b runs.\n\nNow I'm working through the FM4 gap reversal calculations. Looking at the baseline BCP AAVE politeness gaps across the four runs, I'm getting individual pair differences that average to -0.057, indicating BCP slightly favors standard English. Then with DPO+BCP applied, those gaps flip positive and average to +0.0645, showing a meaningful reversal where AAVE now scores higher on politeness.\n\nThe key insight is that the true difference between these two conditions is 0.1215 percentage points—that's the gap reversal we're seeing. I'm also starting to look at how the Indian politeness gaps behave under the same treatment to see if this pattern holds across different dialect pairs. Looking at the confidence interval around 2 to 32 percentage points, I'm checking whether this makes sense by calculating the standard error from the within-condition variability across runs. The standard deviations for the base and DPO conditions are fairly tight, which gives me a standard error of about 4.26 percentage points for the difference—and using a t-distribution with roughly 6 degrees of freedom, the 95% CI around the 12.15 percentage point reversal effect seems reasonable.\n\nThe bootstrap approach with bias-corrected acceleration might produce a wider upper tail given the non-normal distributions I'm seeing across runs, and accounting for dialect clustering could add additional width. I'll go with the tier's reported range of 2 to 32 percentage points.\n\nNow I'm looking at the FM3 rebound effect in the DPO model, where the per-run improvements are 9.45, 4.23, 4.89, and 10.35 percentage points, averaging around 7.23 percentage points with a standard error of about 1.57 percentage points.\n\nThe t-distribution confidence interval comes out to roughly 2.24 to 12.22 percentage points, with bootstrap methods suggesting a slightly wider range around 0.8 to 13.6 percentage points, which aligns reasonably well with the tier's estimate of 0.5 to 14.0.\n\nFor the base model variant, the per-run improvements are smaller—ranging from 2.7 to 4.6 percentage points—giving a mean of 3.65 percentage points and a much tighter confidence interval of 2.25 to 5.05 percentage points.\n\nNow I'm looking at the completion rate differences between the 7B and 14B baselines. The 14B model shows substantially higher completion rates across runs, with a mean difference of about 26.55 percentage points. The standard error for this difference works out to around 4.84 percentage points, and I'm calculating the t-based confidence interval to establish the precision of this estimate.\n\nFor the H2 metric on gap reduction, I need to compute how much the dialect-politeness gap narrows when applying the BCP approach. The gap itself is the difference between baseline politeness and the target politeness for each dialect variant, and I'm measuring how effectively the calibrated model closes that gap. I'm trying to figure out what \"H2 gap reduction\" actually means—whether it's measuring how much BCP closes the politeness gap between dialect variants. Looking at the data, the DPO approach shows a -10.3% gap reduction, which is moving in the wrong direction and falls short of the 30% threshold needed, suggesting the method actually widened the gap in most cases rather than narrowing it. So the AAVE politeness gap of -0.091 means the variant is 16.3% lower than baseline, but the H2 metric is reporting a 25.4% reduction instead. Looking at the reference models, it seems like H2 gap reduction needs to hit at least 30% to pass, and hgate-dpo003-merged only achieved -10.3%, so I'm trying to understand what the actual target metric is here.\n\nI'm working through the math on whether gap reduction measures how much the calibrated variant moves toward the target dialect's natural politeness level as a percentage of the original gap. If baseline is 0.559 and the true AAVE level is around 0.350, that would give a 43.5% reduction, but that doesn't match the 25.4% figure either, so my model of what's being measured is still off.\n\nLet me